  "about": {
    "title": "A district between the ghats and the maidan",
    "kicker": "About Chikkamagaluru",
    "image": "assets/coffee-hills.jpg",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Chikkamagaluru is the headquarters of the district of the same name in Karnataka. The town sits on the foothills of the Chandra Drona / Baba Budan Giri range of the Western Ghats, the range that holds Mullayanagiri, the highest peak in the state.",
      "The district is known for shade-grown coffee, shola grassland, Hoysala temples toward the east, and living mathas at Sringeri and Horanadu. Kannada is the language of the place. This companion is an independent reference. It is not the district administration, and it does not sell rooms."
    ]
  },
  "faqs": [
    {
      "q": "Is this an official government website?",
      "a": "No. It is an independent static companion. For notices, tenders, accommodation lists the administration chooses to publish, and current alerts, use chikkamagaluru.nic.in."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where should visitors stay?",
      "a": "This page does not list or book homestays, hotels or resorts. Use the district tourism pages and licensed operators. Accommodation booking is intentionally out of scope."
    },
    {
      "q": "Do I need a permit?",
      "a": "Treks and visits inside Kudremukh National Park and Bhadra Tiger Reserve generally require permission from the Karnataka Forest Department. Rules, fees and closures change. Confirm on aranya.gov.in or the local forest office, never from a blog quote on this page."
    },
    {
      "q": "When is the best time to visit?",
      "a": "Clearer ridge views are typical from November to February. Waterfalls are at their most dramatic in the monsoon, when roads and parks also close. Treat any calendar as weather, not a guarantee."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I reach town?",
      "a": "By road from Bengaluru, Mysuru or Mangaluru; by rail via Chikkamagaluru / Kadur; by air into Mangaluru or Bengaluru. Check KSRTC and Indian Railways for current services."
    },
    {
      "q": "What language is spoken?",
      "a": "Kannada. English is more common in town than on ghat roads. A greeting in Kannada is useful everywhere."
    },
    {
      "q": "What about emergencies?",
      "a": "Dial 112 in India. District helpline and hospital listings are on the administration’s helpline page, which is linked from this companion."
    }
  ],
  "packing": [
    "A warm layer for the peaks, even after a hot afternoon in town.",
    "Shoes with grip; laterite and wet rock are ordinary here.",
    "A full fuel tank before Charmadi, Kudremukh or remote falls.",
    "Offline maps; signal thins on the ridges.",
    "Cash for jeep last-miles and small temple towns; do not assume cards.",
    "A torch, basic first aid, and no loudspeakers on grassland."
  ],
